server: keep shared session objects 8-byte aligned

alloc_shared_object() places each new object right after the last one
in its session block, and the size of a class or window object includes
its extra bytes (cbClsExtra, cbWndExtra), which can be any number. An
object of a size that is not a multiple of 8 left the next one
misaligned, and SHARED_WRITE_BEGIN's 64-bit store-release of the
sequence number then faults on arm64 (STLR needs natural alignment):
wineserver died with an alignment exception and took every Wine process
with it. The Need for Speed: Porsche Unleashed demo's InstallShield setup
did this every time on Haiku arm64 (2 of 2). Round the size up to 8.

--- a/server/mapping.c
+++ b/server/mapping.c
@@ -1426,6 +1426,11 @@
     struct session_object *object;
     mem_size_t size = sizeof(*object) - sizeof(object_shm_t) + max(shm_size, sizeof(object_shm_t));
 
+    /* the next object starts where this one ends, and its sequence number is written with 64-bit
+     * atomic stores, which fault when misaligned on arm64: class and window extra bytes can make
+     * shm_size any size */
+    size = (size + sizeof(LONG64) - 1) & ~(mem_size_t)(sizeof(LONG64) - 1);
+
     if ((object = find_free_session_object( size )))
         list_remove( &object->entry );
     else
